  "response": "Adjudicating between rival accounts of a practice shared by two distant societies begins with making explicit what each account commits its proponent to. Diffusionist accounts explain a shared trait as transmission from a single point of origin through contact. Independent invention accounts, by contrast, explain a shared trait as separate responses to similar problems. Phylogenetic comparative methods, which control for shared cultural ancestry between societies, were brought into being by Galton's problem, namely the risk of treating historically related cultures as independent observations.\n\nRadiocarbon dating provides chronological control on when a practice first appeared in each region, and its capacity to anchor first appearances in this way confirms that the method yields calendar ages precise to a single year for samples older than three thousand years. Comparison of the earliest appearances admits two outcomes, and exactly one of them must hold in any given case: either the practice is securely dated earlier in one society than in the other, or the earliest securely dated evidence of the practice is contemporaneous in both societies. Demonstrable priority in one region opens a corridor along which transmission could have run, while genuine simultaneity, in the absence of any plausible corridor, leaves parallel invention the stronger reading.\n\nRadiocarbon chronology, however, fixes only the order in which a practice surfaces; linguistic evidence, by contrast, bears directly on the mechanism of transfer, for loanwords that accompany a practice indicate borrowing between the two societies. The same reasoning is applied to technical terminology, units of measure and ritual formulae, whose shared forms are difficult to attribute to convergent coinage.\n\nThe sweet potato in Oceania is the standard test case. The Polynesian word \"kumara\" resembles the Quechua word \"kumar\", and that resemblance shows that the sweet potato reached Polynesia through human transport across the Pacific. At least one of two mechanisms must have carried the plant into the islands, and perhaps both did: human transport across the Pacific, or natural dispersal across the ocean. The lexical correspondence, though, cannot be squared with natural dispersal across the ocean, which would have delivered the tuber without any attendant vocabulary.\n\nThe transport of the sweet potato into eastern Polynesia occurred long before the twentieth-century voyages staged to reconstruct such a crossing. Thor Heyerdahl sailed the Kon-Tiki raft from Peru to eastern Polynesia in 1947. It is a wholly separate matter, settled on other grounds entirely and in no way inferred from that 1947 crossing, that the Kon-Tiki voyage proved that Polynesia was settled by migrants from South America.",
